  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nSurface high pressure will build in behind a departing cold front \nleading to a quiet end to the weekend. Skies will be sunny as \ntemperatures climb into the 80s. Based on yesterday's observed highs \nunder sunny skies, temperatures were increased using a blend of NBM \nand NBM 90th percentile. Dew points will be in the 50s to mid 60s, \nso despite the warmer temps, it will feel more comfortable than past \ndays. Most of the overnight period is expected to be dry as well. \nThe nighttime temperatures will drop into the 60s. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nThe start of Monday is expected to be mainly dry, especially for \nthose further east. Some early morning showers will be possible \nahead of the remnants of an MCS out of the Midwest that will track \neast into the region by midday with support of a wave passing \nthrough. As previously mentioned in past discussions, there are \nsigns of an EML associated with this convection though there is \nuncertainty with how far east that will extended. Instability will \nrange from 500 to 1000 J/kg though with increasing sky cover in the \nmorning, it may be difficult to get values on the higher end of that \nrange. Shear will be fairly weak at 25 to 35 kts. Conditions will be \nmarginal for isolated stronger storms. The main concern on Monday \nwill be the potential for localized heavy rainfall. Model soundings \nshow moist profiles with skinny CAPE. PWATs will be 1.7 to 2 inches. \nUpshear flow will be weak for part of the day, suggesting the \npossibility for slow moving storms. While having a dry day prior \nwill help, any areas that have received higher amounts of rainfall \nin recent days will be most sensitive to isolated hydro issues.\n\nWeak high pressure will briefly build into the region early Tuesday \nand bring drier air over the region. The next disturbance will drop \ninto western NY/PA and clip southern portions of the region Tuesday \nwith showers and thunderstorms. After a brief lull in activity, a \nsurface frontal system will approach the region from the north. \nAdditional showers and thunderstorms will kick off ahead of the \nfront as an upper level trough begins to dig over the Northeast on \nWednesday. This front will sweep through during the overnight hours \nand be followed by drier air. However, with the trough hanging \naround, embedded weak waves will support isolated to scattered \nshowers/storms on Thursday. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\n\nEnsembles are favoring a pattern change at the end of the week as \nthe trough slides east and the ridge over the Midwest builds in. \nThere are some uncertainties on timing though, so if the trough is \nslower to move out, there would be potential for spotty showers \nFriday. Otherwise, drier conditions are favored into the weekend. \nThe ECMWF has been consistent in the past few longer-range runs with \na system moving through on Sunday as the ridge breaks down while \nsolutions like the GFS keep conditions dry under the ridge. Given \nthis, low chance PoPs were introduced for Sunday.",
